# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Kept at medium. The code and validation evidence support a real runtime vulnerability: the debug endpoint is unauthenticated, pre-admission, and reaches mutating storage checkpoint/compaction code. However, the exploit path is localhost-only in the standard binary and requires ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT to be explicitly enabled, so it should not be rated high or critical. The most credible impact is service-level availability degradation rather than confidentiality compromise, account takeover, or remote arbitrary code execution.
## Likelihood
low - Exploitation is straightforward once reachable because it is an unauthenticated HTTP POST with attacker-controlled pg_id, and validation demonstrated it bypasses admission. Likelihood is reduced substantially because the endpoint is disabled by default and the standard binary restricts it to loopback listeners, requiring local or colocated access.
## Impact
medium - Impact is primarily availability: unauthenticated local clients can force synchronous checkpoint and compaction work in the storage layer outside configured request backpressure. There is also minor internal metadata integrity impact because checkpoint/retention metadata is mutated, but no evidence of object data disclosure, credential compromise, RCE, or permanent data loss.
## Assumptions
- The standard argmin-s3 binary and its environment-driven configuration are representative of production/runtime deployments.
- A realistic attacker for this path is an unprivileged local process, local user, colocated workload, or other entity able to connect to the same host/network namespace loopback listener.
- No external cloud APIs or runtime deployment state were queried; exposure is inferred from repository code and configuration defaults.
- ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=true
- argmin-s3 process role includes a frontend
- standard argmin-s3 configuration must bind ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR to a loopback socket when the debug endpoint is enabled
- attacker can send HTTP requests from the local host or same network namespace
## Path
Local attacker -> 127.0.0.1:9000 HTTP -> /__argmin/debug/metadata-checkpoint/record/<pg_id> -> local_debug_response before semaphore/auth -> StorageCluster checkpoint -> command-log compaction -> service availability impact
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:687-696` - local_debug_response is dispatched and returned before normal admission/backpressure handling
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:710-715` - normal request semaphore acquisition occurs only after the debug dispatch path
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1037-1053` - unauthenticated POST debug endpoint parses pg_id from the path and calls the storage checkpoint routine
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1054-1085` - endpoint returns the maintenance result directly without an authorization check
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4373-4424` - called storage routine records a metadata checkpoint and invokes command-log compaction
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:322-325` - ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults to false
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:444-461` - standard binary requires frontend role and loopback listener when the debug endpoint is enabled
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:211` - default listener is 127.0.0.1:9000
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1995-2002` - argmin-s3 binds the configured TCP listener used by the HTTP service
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:4866-4898` - in-repository async test demonstrates the debug endpoint succeeds with request slots set to 0, proving admission bypass reachability
## Narrative
The finding is real and in-scope. In serve.rs, local debug requests are handled at lines 687-696 before the request semaphore is acquired at lines 710-715. The added POST /__argmin/debug/metadata-checkpoint/record/<pg_id> branch parses attacker-controlled path input and directly calls record_current_metadata_command_checkpoint_for_pg() without SigV4 or operator authorization. The storage method records checkpoint state and invokes metadata command-log compaction, confirming a mutating storage path. Exposure is materially limited: ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT defaults to false, and argmin-s3 rejects enabling it unless the listener is loopback. Therefore this is not a remote internet auth bypass or data-exfiltration issue, but it remains a plausible local availability/admission-control vulnerability when the debug endpoint is intentionally enabled.
## Controls
- ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT feature/configuration flag disables the endpoint by default
- argmin-s3 enforces loopback ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR when local debug is enabled
- normal S3 requests use SigV4 authentication, but this local debug path does not
- normal requests use max_inflight_requests semaphore, but this endpoint is handled before that semaphore
## Blindspots
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker Compose, or ingress manifests were found in the checkout, so deployment exposure beyond code defaults could not be verified.
- Static analysis cannot quantify how much repeated checkpoint/compaction calls degrade performance in a real production dataset.
- The temporary validation PoC output was supplied by the earlier validation stage; this attack-path pass relied on repository artifacts and did not rerun long dynamic tests.
